```sql
-- without an index, this scans every row
SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'a@x.com';

-- create an index on the lookup column
CREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email);

-- now the lookup uses the B-tree (O(log n))
SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'a@x.com';
```

*Conceptually: a 1,000,000-row table goes from ~1,000,000 row reads to ~20 B-tree steps.*

```sql
EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'a@x.com';
-- MySQL: type=ALL, rows=1000000  -> full table scan (no usable index)

CREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email);

EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'a@x.com';
-- MySQL: type=ref, key=idx_users_email, rows=1 -> index used

-- get real timings:
EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'a@x.com';
```

*`type` going from `ALL` to `ref` (and `rows` from 1,000,000 to 1) confirms the index works.*

```sql
-- the PRIMARY KEY is usually the clustered index:
CREATE TABLE orders (
  id INT PRIMARY KEY,         -- clustered: rows stored sorted by id
  customer_id INT,
  total DECIMAL(10,2)
);
-- range scans on id are fast because rows are physically contiguous
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id BETWEEN 100 AND 200;
```
